A Set is a part of the Java Collection Framework that stores unique elements (no duplicate values allowed).

👉 It belongs to java.util package.
👉 It does not maintain index like List.
👉 It does not allow duplicate elements.

1. HashSet

Definition:
Stores elements using a hash table.

Features:
Does NOT maintain insertion order
Allows one null value
Fast performance (O(1))

2. LinkedHashSet

Definition:
Maintains insertion order.

Features:
Keeps elements in the order they were inserted
Allows one null
lightly slower than HashSet




3. TreeSet

Definition:
Stores elements in sorted (ascending) order.

Features:
Automatically sorts elements
Does NOT allow null
Slower than HashSet (O(log n))



🔹 What is Synchronization in Java?
Synchronization is a process that controls the access of multiple threads to shared resources.
In simple words:
👉 It makes sure that only one thread can access a critical section (shared data) at a time.





What is Iterator in Java?

An Iterator is an interface in Java that is used to traverse (loop through) elements of a collection one by one.
It is available in the java.util package.